// Phase 5, part 1: the N-thread combination argument's missing piece.
//
// real_compiler_block_reduce.vpr proves ONE generic thread, parameterized
// by tid, behaves safely GIVEN it holds acc(cells[tid].val) permanently and
// is granted a clean acquire/release of its partner cell (tid+stride) each
// round. That is N separate, independent Hoare triples -- not yet a
// concurrency proof. Concurrent Separation Logic's parallel-composition
// rule says N such triples combine into "safe when run together" PROVIDED
// their resource claims are pairwise disjoint at every point. The one
// thing not yet checked anywhere: that disjointness, globally, across all
// 8 threads at once, for every round the real kernel's loop runs.
//
// The crux, independent of which concrete stride value: the set of cells
// being WRITTEN this round ({t : t < stride}, each active thread writing
// its own cell) and the set of cells being READ this round
// ({t + stride : t < stride}, each active thread's partner) must never
// overlap -- if they did, some thread's write would race some OTHER
// thread's read (or, since the active-write-set only touches ITS OWN
// index, with another active thread's write). This is exactly what a
// quantified-permission precondition's own well-formedness check proves:
// Viper requires the receiver set of `forall t :: cond(t) ==> acc(e(t))`
// (and, for a conjunction of several such clauses, the UNION of their
// receiver sets) to be injective -- no two different (clause, t) pairs
// may ever name the same heap location. Phase 0 already showed Silicon
// catches a violation of exactly this (`bad_non_injective_redistribution`);
// this file states the REAL redistribution real_compiler_block_reduce.vpr
// relies on, generically over every stride the loop's own invariant
// allows (1 <= stride <= 4, matching that proof's `invariant v9 <= 4`
// exactly), and confirms Silicon accepts it -- then confirms it correctly
// rejects a deliberately wrong redistribution function, the same
// off-by-one shape as verify_demo_broken.

field val: Int

// `stride` is left symbolic, bounded exactly as the per-thread loop's own
// invariant establishes it is at every check -- so this isn't "checked for
// stride=4, 2, 1" as three separate facts, it's checked once, for every
// value the real loop could ever be examined at.
method round_redistribution_is_injective(cells: Seq[Ref], stride: Int)
  requires |cells| == 8
  requires forall i: Int, j: Int :: 0 <= i && i < 8 && 0 <= j && j < 8 && i != j ==> cells[i] != cells[j]
  requires stride >= 1 && stride <= 4
  requires forall t: Int :: 0 <= t && t < stride ==>
             acc(cells[t].val) && acc(cells[t + stride].val)
{
}

// Deliberately broken: the same off-by-one bug as verify_demo_broken
// (reading one past the real partner cell). At stride=4, t=3: partner
// would be 3+4+1=8 -- out of the declared `cells` range entirely, which
// Viper rejects as its own, separate error (an out-of-bounds sequence
// index) before injectivity is even in question. To isolate the
// injectivity check specifically (not just bounds), this uses `t+1`
// instead: at stride=2, t=0's "partner" becomes cells[1] -- which is
// thread 1's OWN cell, and thread 1 is ALSO active this round (1 < 2).
// Two different t values (t=0's partner slot, t=1's own slot) then name
// the same ref: exactly the double-claim a real race would be.
method round_redistribution_broken_off_by_one(cells: Seq[Ref], stride: Int)
  requires |cells| == 8
  requires stride >= 1 && stride <= 4
  requires forall t: Int :: 0 <= t && t < stride ==>
             acc(cells[t].val) && acc(cells[t + 1].val)
{
}
